  "style": {
    "name": "Indo-Persian sun-temple (Vermilion Dawn)",
    "rationale": "Second pass: reversed the v-prev teal-monochrome regression by restoring RED SANDSTONE as the ~60% dominant body and demoting dark prismarine back to dome+string-courses only; killed the two-gold-eyes 'face' read by collapsing the front into ONE deep central pishtaq with a tapered pointed iwan instead of paired bright windows; integrated the corner pillars into the body as engaged quoined pylons (bonded, projecting relief, no sky between); carved real recessed niche-pairs and a 3-deep iwan by exclusion (front skin built AROUND the openings onto a back plane) rather than flat color; added regular same-family grain — quoin bands, smooth/chiselled coursing, teal string-courses — to every wide field while leaving gold/lapis accents single and saturated so contrast holds; gave the onion dome a belly that overhangs the constricted drum so it never reads as a pyramid."
  },
